>work but with _no other objects_).  Here's the recipe:

This is really cool.  In the old days, before they made it an exception to the
Law of Conservation of Charge, wands of wishing created by polymorph would
have as many charges as before the polymorph.  Thus one could polymorph a wand
of light(13) into a wand of wishing (13).  This made it possible to, with a
pile of only ten or fifteen wands, to get infinite wishes by "plowing under"
(wishing for wands of light and then polymorphing with) the first few WoWs
created.  They fixed this loophole by making sure all WoWs created by
polymorph have only 3 charges.  Nice to see that someone has found a way to
circumvent this.

<description of technique deleted>

>In light of this discovery, I think that the game should be changed to prevent
>"breeding" WoWs.  I have two suggestions for how this could be implemented.
>The simplest would be to prevent polymorphed wands from becoming WoWs.  

No, the simplest is to make minotaurs ungenocidable.  They're "special"
monsters anyway, so this is perfectly consistent.  This should definitely be
in patchlevel 5!  Also, it might be a good idea for the writers of nethack to
start paying a LOT more attention to such violations of the Law of
Conservation of Objects.

>This would probably make the game too difficult to win.

Not really.  Any polymorph pile worth its salt doesn't depend on wands of
wishing.  After I posted that I didn't think you could win 3.0 without
polymorphing at all, I got mail from someone who said he had done this.  When
you start inventing obstacles to make the game harder for yourself, it
definitely is time to "retire and move to Florida," as one previous poster
suggested.

